Three durable cover-letter rules Nick confirmed on the 2026-06-25 batch:

1. **Lead with power, not vulnerability.** The opening sentence asserts strength; it never hedges or apologises. Cut "I want to grow into", "I will be upfront that I have not...", "I am asking you to take a chance on X". Open by stating who he is and what he brings, e.g. "I am an engineer who has worked across product, delivery, and stakeholder engagement, and through my own engagements I have built a far broader skill set I want to bring into my next role with expanded scope."

2. **Do not advertise gaps.** No "honest note" paragraphs volunteering weaknesses (language not primary, no formal title, etc.). Sell why the experience transfers; let the reader surface gaps in interview. He does not need to flag them in writing.

3. **Personal/founder builds framed generically.** Reference "my own projects and not-for-profit work" — not time-bound ("for the past 18 months") and by default not naming the specific product (AccessBuddy). Keeps it supplementary.

**Why:** Vulnerability and volunteered gaps read as weak and hand the reader a reason to screen him out; he sells better on transfer and strength. **How to apply:** Extends [[feedback-cover-letter-no-role-pointing]] and [[feedback-cover-letter-voice]]; mirrored in the career-pivot project file positioning rules. The challenger close ("I would back myself in front of you; if you meet me and disagree, that is fair") is still wanted — confidence, not hedging.
